{
  "abstract": "For a field K, the tensor rank R_K(<n,l,m>) is the least number r of products in a bilinear algorithm A*B = sum_{k<r} alpha_k(A) beta_k(B) C_k for the product of an n x l and an l x m matrix over K (alpha_k, beta_k linear forms, C_k fixed matrices). This entry proves in Lean 4 with Mathlib that R_K(<4,2,4>) = 26 for every field K, with no assumption on the characteristic, finiteness or algebraic closedness of K. The upper bound 26 is the value for p = n = 4 of the formula ceil((3pn + max(n,p))/2) of Hopcroft and Kerr (Cornell Technical Report 69-44, 1969, Theorem 1; SIAM J. Appl. Math. 20 (1971)); here it is proved by an explicit algorithm with 26 products and integer coefficients in {-1, 0, 1}, found by the project's own search and checked by the Lean kernel. In that report Hopcroft and Kerr conjectured (pp. 27-28) that their algorithm is optimal for all p and n, which for <4,2,4> means 26 multiplications; they proved lower bounds only for 2 x 2 times 2 x n and 3 x 2 times 2 x 3, for programs with integer constants and without commutativity, by reduction modulo 2. To our knowledge, a proof of the lower bound 26 is new (literature search recorded in lit/ of this repository); the previously known proved lower bound was 25 (J. Yang, arXiv:2609.14393, Theorem 2, which the development also formalises). The proof of the lower bound builds on Yang's normal form (N1), (N2) for <n,2,m> and extends it with new steps. Lengths below 25 are excluded by Yang's Theorem 2. A decomposition of length 25 is put into a generic normal form over the field of rational functions K(S, T) and read over its algebraic closure. There, Theorem A leaves four rank cases (3,6), (4,7), (5,7), (5,8); Theorem B excludes (4,7) and (5,7) and shows that (3,6) is symmetric; Theorem C shows that the generic normal form is not symmetric. The case (5,8) splits into two sub-cases: sub-case A is excluded by Theorem E, and in sub-case B Theorem F, with Lemma Z and the transposed decomposition, forces a symmetry, which Theorem C excludes. The work was carried out by AI models directed by the repository owner; no human mathematician has reviewed the proof.",
